WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.800
They say that you should set a thief to catch
a thief as no one else understands the criminal

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:05.800
mind better.

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:10.599
Perhaps the most shining example of this idiom
is Vidocq, the man who is now hailed as the

00:00:10.599 --> 00:00:12.969
father of modern criminology.

00:00:12.969 --> 00:00:16.800
He went from a life of crime spent either
on the run or in the prisons of France to

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:21.420
establishing a national detective police force
that served as the model for Scotland Yard

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:22.990
and others that followed it.

00:00:22.990 --> 00:00:27.439
He employed proto-forensic techniques such
as ballistics and casting foot impressions.

00:00:27.439 --> 00:00:32.079
When he left the force, he started his own
agency and became the world's first private

00:00:32.079 --> 00:00:33.079
eye.

00:00:33.079 --> 00:00:36.340
Vidocq goes against many of the tropes we
have of the stereotypical detective.

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:39.890
He was far from brilliant and his success
was more down to him being methodical and

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:40.890
persuasive.

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:43.460
He had a violent streak and was chiefly motivated
by money.

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:47.140
He made plenty of enemies in his career and,
as you are about to see, his life was filled

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:54.039
with almost as many failures as successes.

00:00:54.039 --> 00:01:03.100
Early Days

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:06.680
Most of the information surrounding the detective's
early years comes straight from the horse's

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:11.580
mouth courtesy of the Memoirs of Vidocq, the
autobiography he published with the help of

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:13.799
a ghost-writer around 1828.

00:01:13.799 --> 00:01:18.270
Feel free to take it with a generous helping
of salt.

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:22.520
Eugène François Vidocq was born in the city
of Arras, in the Pas-de-Calais department

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:25.870
in northern France, on July 23, 1775.

00:01:25.870 --> 00:01:31.390
He was the third of seven children of Nicolas
Joseph François Vidocq, a prosperous baker,

00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:33.290
and his wife, Henriette Françoise.

00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:38.210
He was born in the house next to the one where
Maximilien Robespierre was born sixteen years

00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:39.210
prior.

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:40.210
Image suggestion: The Citadel in the Place
d'Armes

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:43.100
The Vidocq family lived in a rough area of
Arras called the Place d'Armes and, consequently,

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:45.820
the young Eugène got into scraps at an early
age.

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:50.800
Fortunately for him, Vidocq had a large, strong
constitution and, by age eight, already bragged

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:53.460
about being the terror of the children in
the neighborhood.

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:57.560
He also took up fencing when he was young
and, by age thirteen, was prodigious with

00:01:57.560 --> 00:01:58.560
the foil.

00:01:58.560 --> 00:01:59.560
First Troubles with the Law

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:03.990
Vidocq's life of crime started as a teenager
and his first target was his own family business.

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:07.920
The youth worked for his father's bakery by
making deliveries around town and would dip

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:10.290
into the till for money to spend at the pub.

00:02:10.290 --> 00:02:15.160
It was also at this time that he began making
the acquaintances of the city's local blackguards,

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:17.690
cons, and other various villains.

00:02:17.690 --> 00:02:20.800
When his father discovered this, he began
locking the till, but the enterprising Vidocq

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:24.020
forged the key with the help of a blacksmith's
son.

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:28.000
When the senior Vidocq, again, put an end
to his son's scheme, the offspring began simply

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.140
stealing items and pawning them.

00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:35.520
This led to his first arrest and a few days
in Baudets, the local jail.

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:41.000
Instead of setting him straight, this experience
convinced Vidocq to go for one big score and

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:42.130
then leave town.

00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:45.580
He broke into the bakery with the help of
a friend and absconded with 2,000 francs.

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:52.670
He split the money in half with his accomplice
and each went their separate ways.

00:02:52.670 --> 00:02:56.610
Life on the Streets

00:02:56.610 --> 00:03:01.370
Early on in his life on the run, Vidocq learned
a harsh, but necessary lesson: there is always

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:02.370
a bigger fish.

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:06.140
He might have considered himself a successful
criminal, but was still naïve in the ways

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:07.140
of the world.

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:11.330
While traveling through Belgium to secure
passage on a ship, he met an amiable stranger

00:03:11.330 --> 00:03:14.220
who took him to a house full of friendly,
beautiful women.

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:18.690
There, he was drugged and he woke up the next
morning on the docks, half-naked, with only

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:20.340
a few crowns to his name.

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:23.920
Vidocq was forced to take employment where
he could find it so he joined a troupe of

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:24.920
performers.

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:29.340
He was treated miserably, beaten regularly
and given scraps for food.

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:32.650
His appearance became so wretched that, at
one point, he was asked to pretend to be a

00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:36.410
savage island cannibal who feasted on raw
flesh.

00:03:36.410 --> 00:03:39.910
This prompted him to leave and he joined a
group of puppeteers, but this new gig did

00:03:39.910 --> 00:03:44.750
not last long as he was kicked out for making
sweet eyes at his employer's wife.

00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:50.560
From there, he hooked up with a traveling
group of peddlers led by one Father Godard.

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:55.260
Back Home

00:03:55.260 --> 00:04:00.210
When opportunity arose, Vidocq returned home
to Arras where his father, eventually, forgave

00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:01.210
him.

00:04:01.210 --> 00:04:05.430
He enlisted in the army and joined the Bourbon
regiment where his reputation and his temper

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:06.840
would often lead to fights.

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:11.430
After six months, Vidocq had earned the nickname
"Reckless" after engaging in fifteen duels

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:13.340
and killing two men.

00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:18.090
In 1792, France entered combat with Austria
in the War of the First Coalition.

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:21.930
Vidocq took part in several battles and was
promoted to grenadier, but deserted after

00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:24.250
receiving a court-martial for hitting an officer.

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:29.220
He joined the 11th Chasseurs under a different
name and, although his desertion was discovered,

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:32.150
a captain interceded on his behalf and all
was forgiven.

00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:35.410
After being shot in the leg, the 18-year-old
Vidocq returned to Arras.

00:04:35.410 --> 00:04:36.410
From One Prison to the Next

00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:39.930
Vidocq's years as a young adult seemed to
have been mostly divided between the prison

00:04:39.930 --> 00:04:41.370
cell and the army.

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:45.720
On the rare occasions that he was free of
both, he typically engaged in romantic trysts

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:47.280
or felonious ventures.

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:51.130
He even married once, but the relationship
ended quickly.

00:04:51.130 --> 00:04:55.000
His adventures took him to Brussels where
he presented himself as Monsieur Rousseau

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:58.320
since Eugène Vidocq was, once again, wanted
for desertion.

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:02.440
In 1795, he joined the flying army and became
a hussar captain.

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:07.220
He caught the eye of an elderly, widowed baroness
who bequeathed him a generous sum that enabled

00:05:07.220 --> 00:05:10.050
Vidocq to live it up in Paris for a while.

00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:13.910
After a few months, the money was gone and
Vidocq was back in prison.

00:05:13.910 --> 00:05:17.650
Here, he discovered a new talent – forgery
– and, with the aid of a lover named Francine

00:05:17.650 --> 00:05:21.410
Longuet, escaped several times, but was always
recaptured soon after.

00:05:21.410 --> 00:05:24.970
In just five years, Vidocq had been imprisoned
in Bicêtre, Douai, Senlis, Brest, and Toulon,

00:05:24.970 --> 00:05:30.030
not to mention once being shanghaied in Rotterdam
and having a short stint as a privateer aboard

00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:31.980
a Dutch ship.

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:34.760
The Turnaround

00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:40.870
In 1800, a 25-year-old Vidocq escaped prison
and made his way back home.

00:05:40.870 --> 00:05:43.061
He found out that his father had died the
previous year.

00:05:43.061 --> 00:05:47.180
He wanted to stay with his mother, but was
recognized and had to flee again.

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:50.960
In Rouen, he assumed the identity of a merchant
named Blondel.

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:55.180
He was successful for a couple of years and
planned to send for his mother before being

00:05:55.180 --> 00:05:58.210
discovered and having to go on the run once
more.

00:05:58.210 --> 00:06:02.380
Time and time again, Vidocq tried to start
a new life in a new city, but it seemed that

00:06:02.380 --> 00:06:06.290
everywhere he went, his past soon caught up
with him.

00:06:06.290 --> 00:06:10.510
In 1809, Vidocq found himself in familiar
surroundings after being arrested and sent

00:06:10.510 --> 00:06:11.980
to the prison in Bicêtre.

00:06:11.980 --> 00:06:13.610
Undoubtedly, he could have escaped.

00:06:13.610 --> 00:06:18.860
He could have used a disguise, or bribed the
guards, or climbed the walls and, eventually,

00:06:18.860 --> 00:06:20.639
Vidocq would have been a free man again.

00:06:20.639 --> 00:06:26.440
However, he had grown weary of prison escapes
and the fleeting liberties they provided.

00:06:26.440 --> 00:06:31.910
He offered to put his experience and reputation
to good use and become a prison informant.

00:06:31.910 --> 00:06:37.020
A police chief named Jean Henry accepted the
deal and relocated Vidocq to La Force Prison

00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:38.020
in Paris.

00:06:38.020 --> 00:06:39.020
Image suggestion: La Force Prison

00:06:39.020 --> 00:06:40.740
It didn't take long for the prisoner to prove
his worth.

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:45.190
Vidocq made the acquaintance of France Tormel,
a criminal who was part of a gang of prolific

00:06:45.190 --> 00:06:46.190
robbers.

00:06:46.190 --> 00:06:49.690
Although already imprisoned, it would not
be long before Tormel was released due to

00:06:49.690 --> 00:06:51.590
lack of evidence or witnesses.

00:06:51.590 --> 00:06:56.080
Vidocq managed to gain his confidence enough
that the felon revealed to him the residence

00:06:56.080 --> 00:06:57.080
of his mistress.

00:06:57.080 --> 00:07:00.960
Sure enough, police conducted a raid and found
enough stolen merchandise to sentence Tormel

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:02.290
to eight years.

00:07:02.290 --> 00:07:06.260
Not only that, but the criminal still trusted
Vidocq and gave him enough information to

00:07:06.260 --> 00:07:08.050
get his accomplices arrested, as well.

00:07:08.050 --> 00:07:11.950
For twenty-one months, Vidocq plied his informant
services between the walls of the prison.

00:07:11.950 --> 00:07:15.590
Eventually, Henry realized that he would be
more useful on the outside as he would be

00:07:15.590 --> 00:07:19.791
able to infiltrate many criminal establishments
instead of just waiting for the criminals

00:07:19.791 --> 00:07:20.791
to come to him.

00:07:20.791 --> 00:07:24.760
They secured his release and made it look
like just another escape.

00:07:24.760 --> 00:07:28.180
Vidocq Is Out

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:34.020
For the first time in a long while, Eugène
Vidocq could walk the streets as himself without

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:38.050
having to worry that the first officer who
recognized him would try to arrest him.

00:07:38.050 --> 00:07:39.580
He wasn't completely a free man, though.

00:07:39.580 --> 00:07:42.270
Vidocq was a secret agent in the service of
the state.

00:07:42.270 --> 00:07:47.560
His goal was to gather information that resulted
in the prevention of crime and capture of

00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:48.560
felons.

00:07:48.560 --> 00:07:50.730
His first collar was a counterfeiter named
Watrin.

00:07:50.730 --> 00:07:55.540
In his autobiography, Vidocq pointed out that
this was the beginning of a problem that dogged

00:07:55.540 --> 00:07:59.850
him his entire time on the force and well
into his career as a private detective.

00:07:59.850 --> 00:08:04.740
All the other officers were jealous of his
success and relished the opportunity to see

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:05.740
him fail.

00:08:05.740 --> 00:08:09.699
Under the old adage of "two heads are better
than one", Vidocq brought in some help and

00:08:09.699 --> 00:08:13.910
organized an informal unit called the Brigade
de la Sûreté (Security Brigade) to aid in

00:08:13.910 --> 00:08:14.910
his crime-fighting.

00:08:14.910 --> 00:08:17.669
It consisted mostly of other reformed criminals
such as himself.

00:08:17.669 --> 00:08:20.680
Back then, France's police force was mainly
divided in two.

00:08:20.680 --> 00:08:25.130
There was the police politique which was the
secret police that handled covert operations

00:08:25.130 --> 00:08:27.580
and uncovered intrigues against the state.

00:08:27.580 --> 00:08:31.389
And there was the standard, man-in-the-street
uniformed police that dealt with common crimes

00:08:31.389 --> 00:08:33.890
like theft, murder, and fraud.

00:08:33.890 --> 00:08:38.210
Crucially, Vidocq convinced his superiors
that his agents should avoid the uniform and,

00:08:38.210 --> 00:08:44.400
instead, don plain clothes and even disguises
to help them infiltrate areas that were, otherwise,

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:46.880
off-limits to police.

00:08:46.880 --> 00:08:50.180
The Sûreté Is Born

00:08:50.180 --> 00:08:52.590
The group was a success.

00:08:52.590 --> 00:08:59.010
Less than a year after it appeared, the Sûreté
officially became a unit of plainclothes detectives

00:08:59.010 --> 00:09:03.880
under the purview of the Prefecture of Police
of Paris with Vidocq as its first chief.

00:09:03.880 --> 00:09:04.880
Image suggestion: Napoleon

00:09:04.880 --> 00:09:09.029
Fortunately for him, the man at the top, Napoleon,
was also a keen reorganizer of France's police

00:09:09.029 --> 00:09:10.029
force.

00:09:10.029 --> 00:09:14.700
He established the Prefecture of Police in
1800 and also increased the numbers and responsibilities

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:20.190
of the gendarmerie who, up until that point,
functioned mainly as a semi-militarized force

00:09:20.190 --> 00:09:23.380
dispatched to keep the peace in rural areas
of the country.

00:09:23.380 --> 00:09:26.861
Seemingly, Bonaparte approved of Vidocq's
revolutionary approach to crime-fighting and,

00:09:26.861 --> 00:09:31.850
a year later, further expanded the Sûreté
into the state security service known as the

00:09:31.850 --> 00:09:33.130
Sûreté Nationale.

00:09:33.130 --> 00:09:35.150
Vidocq trained all of his agents personally.

00:09:35.150 --> 00:09:38.580
Even when he had a few dozen men under him,
he still enjoyed dressing up like a beggar

00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:40.930
and hitting the streets for some undercover
work.

00:09:40.930 --> 00:09:45.240
His unit was responsible for hundreds of arrests
and reportedly decreased the crime rate in

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:46.940
Paris by up to 40 percent.

00:09:46.940 --> 00:09:52.550
According to criminologist John Philip Stead,
in 1817 alone the Sûreté made 811 arrests,

00:09:52.550 --> 00:09:58.800
including 341 thieves, 46 forgers, 14 escaped
prisoners, and 15 assassins.

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:02.600
1817 turned out to be a particularly fruitful
year for Vidocq because he also received a

00:10:02.600 --> 00:10:06.290
full pardon for his past crimes from King
Louis XVIII.

00:10:06.290 --> 00:10:10.500
Up until that point, although he was a superior
officer with the Paris police force, Vidocq

00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:19.010
was still, technically, a wanted criminal.

00:10:19.010 --> 00:10:20.010
The First Retirement

00:10:20.010 --> 00:10:24.260
The following decade proved to be a more turbulent
time for the policeman, both personally and

00:10:24.260 --> 00:10:25.260
professionally.

00:10:25.260 --> 00:10:29.220
He married again in 1820, but his wife passed
away four years later and his mother died

00:10:29.220 --> 00:10:30.220
shortly after.

00:10:30.220 --> 00:10:33.490
In his professional life, Vidocq started to
lose the support of his superiors.

00:10:33.490 --> 00:10:39.290
Jean Henry retired and the next police chief,
Marc Duplessis, was not a fan of the Sûreté.

00:10:39.290 --> 00:10:44.110
Jules Anglès, the police prefect who obtained
a pardon for Vidocq, also had to retire after

00:10:44.110 --> 00:10:47.550
failing to prevent the assassination of Charles
Ferdinand, Duke of Berry.

00:10:47.550 --> 00:10:50.980
Finally, the king himself died and was succeeded
by Charles X.

00:10:50.980 --> 00:10:51.980
Image suggestion: King Charles X

00:10:51.980 --> 00:10:55.801
Vidocq always maintained that the antipathy
showed to him by other members of the police

00:10:55.801 --> 00:10:58.149
force was due to envy of his success.

00:10:58.149 --> 00:11:01.811
While there was certainly plenty of that,
there was also resentment that a criminal

00:11:01.811 --> 00:11:06.420
would advance so high up the ranks without
facing any serious consequences for his illicit

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:07.420
past.

00:11:07.420 --> 00:11:11.720
At the same time, Vidocq became quite wealthy
and famous during his career in law enforcement.

00:11:11.720 --> 00:11:15.000
He rubbed elbows with Parisian elite like
Victor Hugo and Honoré de Balzac and made

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:16.850
enough money to start his own paper company.

00:11:16.850 --> 00:11:21.520
There were unsubstantiated rumors that he
continued his criminal activities, but now

00:11:21.520 --> 00:11:23.279
he just did it with the help of the law.

00:11:23.279 --> 00:11:27.210
By 1827, Vidocq had had one too many confrontations
with his new boss.

00:11:27.210 --> 00:11:31.670
After 18 years on the force, the 52-year-old
tended his resignation to Marc Duplessis.

00:11:31.670 --> 00:11:35.760
The police chief was happy to be rid of him
although, bizarrely, he appointed as his successor

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:38.680
another ex-convict agent named Barthelemy
"Coco" Lacour.

00:11:38.680 --> 00:11:39.850
A Short Return and a Second Retirement

00:11:39.850 --> 00:11:43.290
Vidocq might have been a shrewd criminal and
a pioneering detective, but he was not a very

00:11:43.290 --> 00:11:44.290
good businessman.

00:11:44.290 --> 00:11:48.070
It did not take long before his company went
bankrupt but, on the bright side, the time

00:11:48.070 --> 00:11:50.500
off allowed him to put down his memoirs.

00:11:50.500 --> 00:11:55.920
By 1831, Duplessis was gone and the new police
prefect, Henri Gisquet, invited Vidocq back

00:11:55.920 --> 00:11:56.920
on the force.

00:11:56.920 --> 00:12:00.200
He was chief of the Sûreté again, but his
position was short-lived.

00:12:00.200 --> 00:12:04.891
Perhaps it was merely a symptom of the instability
felt in France during the July Monarchy, or

00:12:04.891 --> 00:12:09.330
maybe due to the cholera outbreak that was
ravaging the country, but people showed little

00:12:09.330 --> 00:12:12.250
indulgence towards Vidocq and his group of
ex-convicts.

00:12:12.250 --> 00:12:16.550
A few instances occurred in short succession
where agents of the Sûreté were accused

00:12:16.550 --> 00:12:21.070
of acting as provocateurs against rioters
and some were even implicated in a theft.

00:12:21.070 --> 00:12:25.800
The unit's actions became harder and harder
to justify and, by the end of 1832, Vidocq

00:12:25.800 --> 00:12:28.399
was once again "cordially invited" to resign.

00:12:28.399 --> 00:12:32.870
He did so by citing concern for the health
of his third wife who, by the way, was also

00:12:32.870 --> 00:12:33.870
his cousin.

00:12:33.870 --> 00:12:37.390
Following his departure, the Sûreté was
purged of anyone who had a criminal record,

00:12:37.390 --> 00:12:38.970
even for minor offences.

00:12:38.970 --> 00:12:44.810
From Spy to Private Eye

00:12:44.810 --> 00:12:46.930
The former agents did not have to look long
for a new job.

00:12:46.930 --> 00:12:53.700
In 1833, Vidocq founded Le Bureau des Renseignements
pour le Commerce ("Office of Information for

00:12:53.700 --> 00:12:54.700
Businesses").

00:12:54.700 --> 00:12:59.480
For a fee, clients could access Vidocq's database
of profiles on crooks and frauds.

00:12:59.480 --> 00:13:03.670
Should the need arise, they could also hire
agents to conduct personal investigations

00:13:03.670 --> 00:13:08.330
and surveillance into their interests which
often had nothing to do with business and,

00:13:08.330 --> 00:13:11.630
instead, pertained to private matters such
as adultery or finances.

00:13:11.630 --> 00:13:16.120
Business information bureaus already existed,
but because of the extra investigation services,

00:13:16.120 --> 00:13:21.010
many consider Vidocq's new endeavor to constitute
the world's first private detective agency.

00:13:21.010 --> 00:13:25.100
Once again, he predominantly hired ex-convicts,
many of them former agents of the Sûreté

00:13:25.100 --> 00:13:26.779
since he already trained them.

00:13:26.779 --> 00:13:31.220
If the rest of the police force had contempt
for Vidocq before, now they loathed him.

00:13:31.220 --> 00:13:35.200
Many were not pleased with the idea of an
extrajudicial group investigating crimes for

00:13:35.200 --> 00:13:36.200
the highest bidder.

00:13:36.200 --> 00:13:40.350
Accusations were thrown now more than ever
before that Vidocq and his detectives operated

00:13:40.350 --> 00:13:45.170
in a grey area of the law, at best, and did
not shy away from a few illegalities to finish

00:13:45.170 --> 00:13:46.170
the job.

00:13:46.170 --> 00:13:50.140
You can't argue with results, though, and
the agency flourished at first.

00:13:50.140 --> 00:13:54.660
However, his quarrels with the true police
force led to several arrests and seizures.

00:13:54.660 --> 00:13:59.230
The new police prefect, Gabriel Delessert,
was definitely not a fan of Vidocq and always

00:13:59.230 --> 00:14:02.140
looked for a new chance to crush his business.

00:14:02.140 --> 00:14:07.830
That opportunity arrived in 1842 when the
prefect ordered a massive raid on the agency

00:14:07.830 --> 00:14:09.860
which resulted in Vidocq's arrest.

00:14:09.860 --> 00:14:12.570
He stood accused of fraud and illegal arrest.

00:14:12.570 --> 00:14:13.570
Image suggestion: Conciergerie

00:14:13.570 --> 00:14:16.730
Eventually, Vidocq was free again, but not
before spending 11 months in the prison of

00:14:16.730 --> 00:14:17.730
the Conciergerie.

00:14:17.730 --> 00:14:20.320
When he was released, he was 67 years old
and it became pretty clear to him that his

00:14:20.320 --> 00:14:21.840
best days were far behind him.

00:14:21.840 --> 00:14:27.670
He spent the rest of his life mostly out of
the limelight and died in 1857.

00:14:27.670 --> 00:14:33.240
Elementary, My Dear Vidocq

00:14:33.240 --> 00:14:36.940
Vidocq laid the groundwork for the calling
of the detective, but we should be clear that

00:14:36.940 --> 00:14:39.220
he was never a Sherlock Holmes-type character.

00:14:39.220 --> 00:14:43.481
He did not fit our image of the typical brilliant
detective who gives brainy summations in a

00:14:43.481 --> 00:14:48.550
room full of suspects and reveals how the
murderer did it while chewing on a lit pipe.

00:14:48.550 --> 00:14:52.360
If anything, he was a more successful psychologist
than detective.

00:14:52.360 --> 00:14:56.700
He knew how to use his appearance, his reputation,
and his attitude to get other criminals to

00:14:56.700 --> 00:14:58.950
trust and confide in him.

00:14:58.950 --> 00:15:02.950
Another dull, but practical ace up his sleeve
was his obsession with keeping accurate and

00:15:02.950 --> 00:15:05.879
detailed records on all the criminals he encountered.

00:15:05.879 --> 00:15:10.210
Vidocq often boasted of having an incredible
memory, but he knew that his agents were not

00:15:10.210 --> 00:15:11.210
as gifted.

00:15:11.210 --> 00:15:15.740
Early in its infancy, the Sûreté began making
index cards for the rogues of Paris, listing

00:15:15.740 --> 00:15:19.760
their physical appearance, distinguishing
traits, prior convictions, modus operandi,

00:15:19.760 --> 00:15:22.150
and any other information that might prove
useful.

00:15:22.150 --> 00:15:26.960
The system was primitive, but it worked, and
it came in real handy later when Vidocq started

00:15:26.960 --> 00:15:28.170
his private agency.

00:15:28.170 --> 00:15:32.959
The Sûreté made use of it for decades until
it was overhauled and improved by Alphonse

00:15:32.959 --> 00:15:33.959
Bertillon.

00:15:33.959 --> 00:15:37.410
This being said, let's not completely detract
from the man's accomplishments as Vidocq put

00:15:37.410 --> 00:15:41.970
his talents to good use in ways very few other
crime-fighters had done up until that point.

00:15:41.970 --> 00:15:46.959
He's had, at least, a handful of cases that
would serve well for a Sunday afternoon crime

00:15:46.959 --> 00:15:47.959
show.

00:15:47.959 --> 00:15:51.360
On one occasion, Vidocq met early one morning
with a suspected thief named Hotot.

00:15:51.360 --> 00:15:54.600
The latter looked tired, his clothes were
wet and his boots were muddy.

00:15:54.600 --> 00:15:57.080
Clearly, this was a man who had had a busy
night.

00:15:57.080 --> 00:16:01.870
Vidocq excused himself and went to inquire
about burglaries that happened the night before.

00:16:01.870 --> 00:16:05.890
He arrived at a crime scene and noticed a
pair of footprints which made him think of

00:16:05.890 --> 00:16:06.930
Hotot's muddy boots.

00:16:06.930 --> 00:16:11.400
He had an agent fetch some plaster of Paris
and he made a cast of the prints which he

00:16:11.400 --> 00:16:13.380
then matched to the criminal's boots.

00:16:13.380 --> 00:16:15.670
Hotot confessed and was sent to the galley.

00:16:15.670 --> 00:16:16.670
Image suggestion: Cap and ball pistol

00:16:16.670 --> 00:16:20.839
Another time, Vidocq investigated the notorious
murder of Countess Isabelle d'Arcy.

00:16:20.839 --> 00:16:25.110
Everybody assumed her husband had shot her,
but the detective relied on a primitive form

00:16:25.110 --> 00:16:27.510
of ballistics to prove the count innocent.

00:16:27.510 --> 00:16:30.900
He had the doctor extract the bullet from
the countess's body – something he had to

00:16:30.900 --> 00:16:35.300
do in secret because it was highly irregular
for that time – and showed that the ball

00:16:35.300 --> 00:16:38.870
was too large to fit in Count d'Arcy's dueling
pistol.

00:16:38.870 --> 00:16:42.839
Instead, Vidocq matched it to the weapon of
the countess's lover who confessed.

00:16:42.839 --> 00:16:47.550
Lunch with a Side of Murder

00:16:47.550 --> 00:16:49.720
Image suggestion: Jean Valjean/Javert side-by-side

00:16:49.720 --> 00:16:52.740
Eugène Vidocq might be long gone, but his
legacy lives on.

00:16:52.740 --> 00:16:56.810
His literary friends immortalized him in their
books as Balzac based several characters on

00:16:56.810 --> 00:16:59.480
him, chief among them the criminal mastermind
Vautrin.

00:16:59.480 --> 00:17:04.160
In his seminal work, Les Misérables, Victor
Hugo drew inspiration for both of his main

00:17:04.160 --> 00:17:07.481
characters, the convict Jean Valjean and the
policeman Javert, from the dual life of Vidocq.

00:17:07.481 --> 00:17:12.519
The detective himself left behind quite a
complimentary account of his exploits in the

00:17:12.519 --> 00:17:13.659
form of his memoirs.

00:17:13.659 --> 00:17:17.860
But perhaps no other tribute is more fitting
than the Vidocq Society.

00:17:17.860 --> 00:17:21.069
Back in September 1990, three criminal experts
met for lunch.

00:17:21.069 --> 00:17:25.740
William Fleisher was a retired Philadelphia
police officer and FBI agent, Frank Bender

00:17:25.740 --> 00:17:29.820
was a forensic sculptor, and Richard Walter
was a criminal psychologist.

00:17:29.820 --> 00:17:33.669
They started talking shop and the discussion,
eventually, turned to cold cases.

00:17:33.669 --> 00:17:37.360
The three men realized that their knowledge
and experience could prove to be an asset

00:17:37.360 --> 00:17:42.100
to a detective who is stuck looking for that
one little clue that could lead to a break

00:17:42.100 --> 00:17:43.100
in the case.

00:17:43.100 --> 00:17:46.820
Not only that, but they knew plenty of other
people who could also lend their expertise.

00:17:46.820 --> 00:17:48.809
Thus, the Vidocq Society was born.

00:17:48.809 --> 00:17:49.809
How It Works

00:17:49.809 --> 00:17:53.149
The organization meets once a month at the
Union League of Philadelphia.

00:17:53.149 --> 00:17:57.580
It has 82 permanent members (one for each
year that Vidocq lived), although many others

00:17:57.580 --> 00:18:00.029
have joined as Special members by invitation
only.

00:18:00.029 --> 00:18:03.859
They include forensic scientists, law enforcement
officers, prosecutors, profilers, medical

00:18:03.859 --> 00:18:05.799
examiners, and psychologists.

00:18:05.799 --> 00:18:09.379
Seldom will you find more experts on crime
gathered under the same roof.

00:18:09.379 --> 00:18:13.940
The members have lunch and listen to an investigator
who is there to present a murder case.

00:18:13.940 --> 00:18:14.940
He is stumped.

00:18:14.940 --> 00:18:17.820
He is out of leads and the trail has gone
cold for years.

00:18:17.820 --> 00:18:21.320
He is hoping that the people there might have
some extra insight.

00:18:21.320 --> 00:18:25.460
Members of the Vidocq Society offer guidance
and advice and, in certain cases, even travel

00:18:25.460 --> 00:18:29.679
to the officer's jurisdiction to take a more
hands-on approach with the investigation.

00:18:29.679 --> 00:18:34.710
Over the years, the society has concentrated
its efforts on cold cases from small or underfunded

00:18:34.710 --> 00:18:37.960
police precincts where its efforts would have
the largest impact.

00:18:37.960 --> 00:18:38.960
The Legacy Continued

00:18:38.960 --> 00:18:41.700
The society first tasted success in March
of 1991.

00:18:41.700 --> 00:18:45.419
The family of a murder victim named Huey Cox
from Little Rock, Arkansas, believed that

00:18:45.419 --> 00:18:47.259
the man charged with the crime was innocent.

00:18:47.259 --> 00:18:48.259
Vidocqians agreed.

00:18:48.259 --> 00:18:52.340
Two of them provided expert testimony at the
trial and the case was dismissed.

00:18:52.340 --> 00:18:54.340
Almost 30 years have passed since then.

00:18:54.340 --> 00:18:59.169
The Vidocq Society helped solve cases like
the Arizona Canal Murders and family annihilator

00:18:59.169 --> 00:19:00.269
John List.

00:19:00.269 --> 00:19:04.450
The group stresses that it doesn't want fame
or glory and, indeed, often there is none.

00:19:04.450 --> 00:19:09.410
The society laments that not enough investigators
are actually aware of their services and would

00:19:09.410 --> 00:19:14.210
like a bit more attention if only so that
it can work more cases and continue the detective

00:19:14.210 --> 00:19:39.980
legacy 
of Eugène Vidocq.

